---
import NoteLayout from '../../layouts/NoteLayout.astro';
import Tex from '../../components/Tex.astro';
---
<NoteLayout slug="polyconvexity"
  tldr="An elastic energy cannot be convex in the deformation gradient, so polyconvexity asks instead for convexity in its minors (entries, cofactors and determinant), a condition you can check by hand that keeps what convexity was needed for; tested on the common materials of my SCA 2026 table, only the Neo-Hookean variants survive, because being convex in the principal stretches is not enough."
  source="This note grows out of two of my papers. My SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 paper fits stretch-based (Valanis–Landel) materials to measured data and enforces their stability through convexity in the stretches; my SCA 2026 paper tabulates the common isotropic materials of graphics and mechanics. Re-checking those materials against the conditions below is what this note adds.">

  <h2 id="question">The question</h2>
  <p>
    A hyperelastic material is described by a stored energy density <Tex tex={String.raw`W(\mathbf{F})`} />, a function of
    the deformation gradient <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}`} />. For the elastic problem to have a solution at all, and
    for a simulation to behave, <Tex tex={String.raw`W`} /> needs some kind of convexity. The obvious guess, "convex in
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}`} />", turns out to be wrong. This note is about why, about the replacement that
    nonlinear elasticity settled on, and about which of the materials we use every day actually satisfy it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-convex">Why not just convex?</h2>
  <p>
    Two things every reasonable material must do are enough to rule it out. It must not care about rigid rotations,
    <Tex tex={String.raw`W(\mathbf{QF}) = W(\mathbf{F})`} /> for every rotation <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{Q}`} />; and
    it must resist being crushed, <Tex tex={String.raw`W(\mathbf{F})\to\infty`} /> as <Tex tex={String.raw`\det\mathbf{F}\to 0^+`} />.
    In 2D, the matrix <Tex tex={String.raw`-\mathbf{I}`} /> is a rotation by 180 degrees, so
    <Tex tex={String.raw`W(-\mathbf{I}) = W(\mathbf{I})`} />. If <Tex tex={String.raw`W`} /> were convex, the midpoint of
    those two matrices would cost at most their average:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`W(\mathbf{0}) = W\!\left(\tfrac12\,\mathbf{I} + \tfrac12\,(-\mathbf{I})\right) \le \tfrac12 W(\mathbf{I}) + \tfrac12 W(-\mathbf{I}) = W(\mathbf{I}).`} />
  <p>
    But <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}=\mathbf{0}`} /> squashes everything to a point; it should cost an infinite amount,
    not less than the rest state. (In 3D the same argument works with the 180-degree rotation
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1)`} />.) The problem is that the set of matrices with positive
    determinant is not convex, and rotations live all over it. Convexity would also be too strong in a second way: a
    strictly convex energy has at most one equilibrium under dead loads, so it could never buckle. Ball (1977) discusses
    both points.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rank-one">The weakest sensible condition: rank-one convexity</h2>
  <p>
    Instead of asking for convexity along every straight line in matrix space, ask only along lines in
    <em>rank-one</em> directions:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`t \;\mapsto\; W(\mathbf{F} + t\,\mathbf{a}\otimes\mathbf{b}) \quad\text{is convex for all }\mathbf{F},\ \mathbf{a},\ \mathbf{b}.`} />
  <p>
    Rank-one directions are special because they are the only jumps a deformation gradient can make across a flat
    interface: two deformations that stay glued together along a plane with normal <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{b}`} />
    can only differ in gradient by some <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{a}\otimes\mathbf{b}`} />. For smooth energies this
    condition is equivalent to <em>ellipticity</em> (the Legendre–Hadamard condition): waves travel at real speeds, and the
    material does not spontaneously form shear bands. It is physically the right minimum. But it is too weak to prove
    that minimizers exist, and Šverák (1992) showed that in three or more dimensions it does not imply the next
    condition.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quasi">The right condition, which nobody can check: quasiconvexity</h2>
  <p>
    What existence proofs actually need is Morrey's quasiconvexity: no wiggle with fixed boundary values can lower the
    energy of a homogeneous deformation,
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\int_\Omega W(\mathbf{F} + \nabla\boldsymbol{\varphi})\,dX \;\ge\; \int_\Omega W(\mathbf{F})\,dX \qquad \text{for all } \boldsymbol{\varphi} \text{ with } \boldsymbol{\varphi} = \mathbf{0} \text{ on } \partial\Omega.`} />
  <p>
    Under suitable growth conditions it is equivalent to the energy being weakly lower semicontinuous, which is the key
    step in proving that a minimizer exists. The catch is the "for all <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\varphi}`} />": it
    is a statement about infinitely many test functions, and there is no general algebraic test for it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="poly">Polyconvexity: convex in the minors</h2>
  <p>
    Ball's (1977) way out was to ask for convexity not in <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}`} /> itself but in the list of its
    minors. In 3D, <Tex tex={String.raw`W`} /> is <em>polyconvex</em> if
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`W(\mathbf{F}) = G\big(\mathbf{F},\ \operatorname{cof}\mathbf{F},\ \det\mathbf{F}\big) \quad\text{with } G \text{ convex};`} />
  <p>
    in 2D the list is just <Tex tex={String.raw`(\mathbf{F}, \det\mathbf{F})`} />. Three facts make this the right
    definition.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It implies rank-one convexity.</strong> Every minor of <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}+t\,\mathbf{a}\otimes\mathbf{b}`} />
      is an <em>affine</em> function of <Tex tex={String.raw`t`} />; for example
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\det(\mathbf{F}+t\,\mathbf{a}\otimes\mathbf{b}) = \det\mathbf{F} + t\,\mathbf{a}\cdot\operatorname{cof}(\mathbf{F})\,\mathbf{b}`} />.
      A convex function of affine functions is convex, so <Tex tex={String.raw`W`} /> is convex along every rank-one line.
      Along a general line, <Tex tex={String.raw`\det`} /> is a polynomial of higher degree, and no such guarantee holds.</li>
    <li><strong>It survives limits.</strong> The minors are <em>null Lagrangians</em>: the integral of
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\det\nabla\boldsymbol{\varphi}`} /> depends only on the boundary values of
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\varphi}`} />. As a result they are weakly continuous, convexity in them passes to
      weak limits, and Ball's existence theorem goes through.</li>
    <li><strong>It allows the barrier.</strong> Because <Tex tex={String.raw`\det\mathbf{F}`} /> is its own variable,
      <Tex tex={String.raw`G`} /> can blow up as it goes to zero. <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{I}`} /> and <Tex tex={String.raw`-\mathbf{I}`} />
      have the same determinant, so the contradiction above disappears.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The resulting hierarchy is
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\text{convex} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{polyconvex} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{quasiconvex} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{rank-one convex},`} />
  <p>
    with some technical care for energies that are infinite at <Tex tex={String.raw`\det\mathbf{F}\le 0`} />. Polyconvexity
    sits at the sweet spot: strong enough for existence, weak enough to allow real materials, and checkable by writing
    <Tex tex={String.raw`W`} /> in the right variables. The practical way to use the hierarchy is in both directions:
    to <em>prove</em> a material is well behaved, write it in the minors; to <em>disprove</em> it, find one rank-one line
    along which it bends down.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">The materials in my SCA 2026 table</h2>
  <p>
    My SCA 2026 paper (with Jernej Barbič) collects the common isotropic materials of graphics and engineering in one
    table, written as functions of the principal stretches <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3`} />
    (its Table 1). That paper is about their small-deformation and weakly nonlinear behavior, not about convexity. So here
    I put the same materials through both tests above: a hand proof where the energy splits into convex functions of the
    minors, and a numerical search for a concave rank-one line where it does not. The search samples 20,000 random lines
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}+t\,\mathbf{a}\otimes\mathbf{b}`} /> in 3D, with principal stretches of
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}`} /> between 0.2 and 3, at <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu=\lambda`} /> (Poisson's ratio 0.25),
    and confirms every hit with several finite-difference step sizes and a direct midpoint test.
  </p>
  <table class="pc-table">
    <tr><th>Material (Table 1)</th><th>Polyconvex?</th><th>Concave rank-one line found?</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Neo-Hookean (Ogden form), <Tex tex={String.raw`\tfrac{\mu}{2}(|\mathbf{F}|^2-3)-\mu\log J+\tfrac{\lambda}{2}(J-1)^2`} /></td><td>yes</td><td>none</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stable Neo-Hookean, <Tex tex={String.raw`\tfrac{\mu}{2}(|\mathbf{F}|^2-3)-\mu(J-1)+\tfrac{\lambda}{2}(J-1)^2`} /></td><td>yes</td><td>none</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Neo-Hookean (standard), volume term <Tex tex={String.raw`\tfrac{\lambda}{2}\log^2 J`} /></td><td>proof covers <Tex tex={String.raw`J\le e^{1+\mu/\lambda}`} /></td><td>none</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Linear Corotational</td><td>no</td><td>yes: shear after squeezing below <Tex tex={String.raw`\tfrac{2\mu+3\lambda}{4\mu+3\lambda}`} /></td></tr>
    <tr><td>St. Venant–Kirchhoff</td><td>no</td><td>yes: uniaxial compression below <Tex tex={String.raw`1/\sqrt3`} /></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hencky</td><td>no</td><td>yes: uniaxial line after a uniform expansion beyond about 1.8</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Volume-corrected LC and StVK, Valanis–Landel (original)</td><td>no</td><td>yes: shear under strong compression</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ARAP, Symmetric ARAP, Symmetric Dirichlet</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    The two polyconvex rows are one argument: <Tex tex={String.raw`|\mathbf{F}|^2`} /> is convex in
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}`} />, and everything else is a convex function of <Tex tex={String.raw`J`} /> alone.
    (Stable Neo-Hookean is convex in <Tex tex={String.raw`J`} /> on the whole real line, including inverted elements.) For
    the standard Neo-Hookean, the volume part <Tex tex={String.raw`-\mu\log J+\tfrac{\lambda}{2}\log^2J`} /> has second
    derivative <Tex tex={String.raw`(\mu+\lambda(1-\log J))/J^2`} />, so the same split proves polyconvexity only up to
    <Tex tex={String.raw`J=e^{1+\mu/\lambda}`} /> (about 7.4 at <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu=\lambda`} />); the search found no
    failure beyond it either, but a search is evidence, not a proof. The "yes" entries in the last column, on the other
    hand, are proofs of failure: one concave rank-one line is enough to rule out polyconvexity.
  </p>
  <p>
    Note the pattern. The materials that pass contain <Tex tex={String.raw`|\mathbf{F}|^2 = \sum_i\lambda_i^2`} />, which
    is increasing in every stretch. Almost all of those that fail are built from a function of each stretch that is
    smallest at <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda=1`} />, such as <Tex tex={String.raw`(\lambda-1)^2`} />. The next section
    explains why that is the culprit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stretches">Convex in the stretches is not enough</h2>
  <p>
    My SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 paper (with Danyong Zhao and Jernej Barbič) fits materials of the Valanis–Landel form
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`W = \sum_{i=1}^3 f(\lambda_i) + h(J), \qquad f(1)=f'(1)=0,`} />
  <p>
    with <Tex tex={String.raw`f''`} /> a piecewise-linear spline whose control values are the fitting parameters. Its
    stability condition is <Tex tex={String.raw`f''>0`} />, which is trivial to enforce on the control values; and to keep
    the Hessian definite far from rest, the spline's <Tex tex={String.raw`f''`} /> is held at positive constants for very
    small and very large stretches. It is tempting to read "<Tex tex={String.raw`f`} /> convex" as enough for good
    behavior everywhere. It is not quite: Linear Corotational is convex in the stretches and still known to be unstable
    under strong compression. Here is the exact reason, and it applies to the whole family.
  </p>
  <p>
    Squeeze a cube uniformly, <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}=s\mathbf{I}`} />, and then shear it,
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}(t)=s\mathbf{I}+t\,\mathbf{e}_1\otimes\mathbf{e}_2`} />. That is a rank-one line, and along
    it the determinant stays at <Tex tex={String.raw`s^3`} />, so the volume term <Tex tex={String.raw`h(J)`} /> drops out
    completely. What remains has second derivative
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\frac{d^2W}{dt^2}\bigg|_{t=0} = \frac{1}{2}\left(f''(s) + \frac{f'(s)}{s}\right) = \frac{\big(s\,f'(s)\big)'}{2s}.`} />
  <p>
    The two terms are the two Hessian eigenvalues that a shear mixes: the "flip" mode
    <Tex tex={String.raw`(f'(\lambda_i)-f'(\lambda_j))/(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)\to f''(s)`} />, and the "twist" mode
    <Tex tex={String.raw`(f'(\lambda_i)+f'(\lambda_j))/(\lambda_i+\lambda_j) = f'(s)/s`} />, the same block-diagonal
    eigenvalues derived in that paper (see also <a href="/notes/isotropic-gradient-hessian">the gradient and Hessian note</a>).
    Convexity of <Tex tex={String.raw`f`} /> makes the flip mode positive. But <Tex tex={String.raw`f`} /> has its minimum
    at <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda=1`} />, so <Tex tex={String.raw`f'(s)<0`} /> for every <Tex tex={String.raw`s<1`} />:
    under compression the twist mode is negative. If <Tex tex={String.raw`f''`} /> stays bounded as
    <Tex tex={String.raw`s\to0`} />, as it does with the constant spline tail, the numerator tends to
    <Tex tex={String.raw`f'(0^+)<0`} />, so <em>every</em> material of this form loses rank-one convexity once it is
    squeezed far enough, whatever <Tex tex={String.raw`h`} /> is. The thresholds are concrete:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><Tex tex={String.raw`f=\mu(\lambda-1)^2`} /> (ARAP, and the volume-corrected Linear Corotational):
      <Tex tex={String.raw`s<1/2`} />.</li>
    <li>Original Valanis–Landel, <Tex tex={String.raw`f=2\mu\big(\lambda(\log\lambda-1)+1\big)`} />:
      <Tex tex={String.raw`s<1/e\approx0.37`} />.</li>
    <li>Linear Corotational, whose <Tex tex={String.raw`\tfrac{\lambda}{2}(\sum\lambda_i-3)^2`} /> term is not a function
      of <Tex tex={String.raw`J`} />: <Tex tex={String.raw`s<(2\mu+3\lambda)/(4\mu+3\lambda)`} />, which is 5/7 at Poisson's
      ratio 0.25. That is a mere 30% squeeze.</li>
    <li>Neo-Hookean, <Tex tex={String.raw`f=\tfrac{\mu}{2}\lambda^2`} />: <Tex tex={String.raw`s\,f'(s)=\mu s^2`} /> is
      increasing, so this line is always convex; its <Tex tex={String.raw`-\mu\log J`} /> lives in
      <Tex tex={String.raw`h`} />.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So the precise statement is: convex <Tex tex={String.raw`f`} /> gives convexity in the stretches and a positive
    "scaling" block, but not polyconvexity. Ball's sufficient condition for energies of this form asks the stretch function
    to be convex <em>and nondecreasing</em>, and a function with its minimum at <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda=1`} /> is not.
    Along this family of lines, the necessary condition is that <Tex tex={String.raw`s\,f'(s)`} /> be nondecreasing. In a
    simulation the loss is easy to miss, because the SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 paper projects each element Hessian to be positive
    semi-definite by clamping exactly these scalar twist eigenvalues at zero (or a small positive value). That keeps Newton's method robust, and it
    also hides the fact that the continuum material has stopped being elliptic.
  </p>

  <h2 id="demo">Try it</h2>
  <p>
    The plot shows energies from the table (3D, <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu=\lambda=1`} />) along a straight line in matrix
    space, with <Tex tex={String.raw`\det\mathbf{F}`} /> along the same line in the strip below. Shaded stretches are where
    a curve bends downward, that is, where convexity along the line fails. <strong>Rank-one line</strong> moves along
    <Tex tex={String.raw`s\mathbf{I}+t\,\mathbf{a}\otimes\mathbf{b}`} /> (the vectors live in the
    <Tex tex={String.raw`x`} />-<Tex tex={String.raw`y`} /> plane; the third stretch stays at <Tex tex={String.raw`s`} />):
    set the squeeze <Tex tex={String.raw`s`} />, then turn <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{a}`} /> and
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{b}`} />. <strong>Line to a rotation</strong> moves from
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{I}`} /> to a rotation by <Tex tex={String.raw`\theta`} />: both ends cost nothing and the
    middle costs something, so no energy can be convex along it; at 180 degrees the line passes through a singular
    matrix.
  </p>
  <figure class="note-fig pc-demo">
    <div class="pc-controls">
      <button type="button" id="pc-r1" aria-pressed="true">Rank-one line</button>
      <button type="button" id="pc-rot" aria-pressed="false">Line to a rotation</button>
    </div>
    <div class="pc-controls" id="pc-pick"></div>
    <div class="pc-controls" id="pc-r1-ctl">
      <label>squeeze <Tex tex={String.raw`s`} /> <input type="range" id="pc-s" min="0.3" max="2" step="0.01" value="1" /> <span id="pc-sv">1.00</span></label>
      <label>direction of <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{a}`} /> <input type="range" id="pc-a" min="0" max="180" step="1" value="0" /> <span id="pc-av">0°</span></label>
      <label>direction of <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{b}`} /> <input type="range" id="pc-b" min="0" max="360" step="1" value="0" /> <span id="pc-bv">0°</span></label>
    </div>
    <div class="pc-controls" id="pc-rot-ctl" hidden>
      <label>rotation angle <Tex tex={String.raw`\theta`} /> <input type="range" id="pc-th" min="0" max="180" step="1" value="120" /> <span id="pc-thv">120°</span></label>
    </div>
    <canvas id="pc-canvas" width="880" height="560" aria-label="Plot of elastic energies and the determinant along a straight line in matrix space."></canvas>
    <div class="pc-read" id="pc-read"></div>
    <figcaption>Each curve is one material from the table; shading in the same color marks where it is concave. The lower
      strip plots <Tex tex={String.raw`\det\mathbf{F}`} />, which is a straight line along rank-one directions and a parabola
      along the line to a rotation.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Three things to try. With <Tex tex={String.raw`s=1`} /> and both directions at 0 degrees the line is uniaxial stretch
    and compression: St. Venant–Kirchhoff turns concave just left of <Tex tex={String.raw`t = 1/\sqrt3 - 1 \approx -0.42`} />,
    while Neo-Hookean rises to infinity as <Tex tex={String.raw`\det\mathbf{F}`} /> reaches zero at
    <Tex tex={String.raw`t=-1`} />. Then set <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{b}`} /> to 90 degrees, a pure shear: at
    <Tex tex={String.raw`s=1`} /> every curve is convex. Now lower the squeeze to 0.6 and watch Linear Corotational turn
    concave at <Tex tex={String.raw`t=0`} /> while Neo-Hookean does not move; switch on ARAP and Valanis–Landel and lower
    <Tex tex={String.raw`s`} /> past 0.5 and 0.37.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checklist">A short checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Write the energy in the minors.</strong> To show polyconvexity, find variables
      <Tex tex={String.raw`(\mathbf{F}, \operatorname{cof}\mathbf{F}, \det\mathbf{F})`} /> in which it is convex; the
      volumetric term is usually the one to check.</li>
    <li><strong>Convex in the stretches is a different property.</strong> For
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\sum_i f(\lambda_i)+h(J)`} />, also check that <Tex tex={String.raw`s\,f'(s)`} /> is
      nondecreasing, or test the twist eigenvalues <Tex tex={String.raw`(f'(\lambda_i)+f'(\lambda_j))/(\lambda_i+\lambda_j)`} />
      under compression.</li>
    <li><strong>Test rank-one lines numerically.</strong> Sampling <Tex tex={String.raw`W(\mathbf{F}+t\,\mathbf{a}\otimes\mathbf{b})`} />
      over many <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{F}, \mathbf{a}, \mathbf{b}`} /> is a cheap way to catch a loss of ellipticity
      before a simulation does. Squeeze-then-shear is the first line to try.</li>
    <li><strong>Hessian projection is a solver fix, not a material fix.</strong> Clamping negative eigenvalues keeps Newton
      stable; it does not make the material elliptic.</li>
    <li><strong>Polyconvex is sufficient, not necessary.</strong> Losing it does not doom a model, but losing rank-one
      convexity does change the physics: the material can localize.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted"><small>References: J. M. Ball, "Convexity conditions and existence theorems in nonlinear elasticity",
    Archive for Rational Mechanics and Analysis 63 (1977), 337–403. C. B. Morrey, "Quasi-convexity and the lower
    semicontinuity of multiple integrals", Pacific Journal of Mathematics 2 (1952). V. Šverák, "Rank-one convexity does not
    imply quasiconvexity", Proceedings of the Royal Society of Edinburgh A 120 (1992), 185–189. H. Chen, D. Zhao,
    J. Barbič, "Capturing Animation-Ready Isotropic Materials Using Systematic Poking", ACM Transactions on Graphics 42(6)
    (SIGGRAPH Asia 2023). H. Chen, J. Barbič, "Weak Nonlinearity and Linear Equalization for Isotropic Hyperelastic
    Materials", ACM SIGGRAPH / Eurographics Symposium on Computer Animation (SCA) 2026. H. Xu, F. Sin, Y. Zhu, J. Barbič,
    "Nonlinear Material Design Using Principal Stretches", ACM Transactions on Graphics 34(4) (SIGGRAPH 2015).</small></p>
</NoteLayout>

<style>
  .pc-table { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1em 0; font-size: .92em; }
  .pc-table td, .pc-table th { padding: .3em .9em .3em 0; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); vertical-align: top; }
  .pc-demo canvas { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; margin-top: .6em; }
  .pc-controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5em 1.2em; align-items: center; margin-top: .5em; font-size: .95em; }
  .pc-controls[hidden] { display: none; }
  .pc-controls button { font: inherit; padding: .3em .8em; cursor: pointer; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px; }
  .pc-controls button[aria-pressed='true'] { border-color: var(--link); color: var(--link); }
  .pc-controls input[type='range'] { vertical-align: middle; }
  .pc-read { margin-top: .6em; font-size: .9em; line-height: 1.5; }
</style>

<script>
  import { MATERIALS, rankOnePath, rotationPath, sample } from '../../scripts/polyconvexity.js';
  const cv = document.getElementById('pc-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const ctx = cv.getContext('2d')!;
  const W = cv.width, H = cv.height;
  const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement;
  let mode: 'r1' | 'rot' = 'r1';
  const css = (v: string) => getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(v).trim() || '#000';
  const deg = Math.PI / 180;
  const COLORS = ['#d62728', '#e377c2', '#1f77b4', '#2ca02c', '#9467bd', '#8c564b', '#ff7f0e', '#17becf'];
  const on = MATERIALS.map((m) => m.on);

  const pick = document.getElementById('pc-pick')!;
  MATERIALS.forEach((m, i) => {
    const lab = document.createElement('label');
    lab.innerHTML = `<input type="checkbox" ${m.on ? 'checked' : ''} /> <span style="display:inline-block;width:1.3em;height:0;border-top:3px solid ${COLORS[i]};vertical-align:middle;margin-right:.3em"></span>${m.name}`;
    lab.querySelector('input')!.addEventListener('change', (e) => { on[i] = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).checked; draw(); });
    pick.appendChild(lab);
  });

  const hexA = (hex: string, a: number) => { const n = parseInt(hex.slice(1), 16); return `rgba(${n >> 16},${(n >> 8) & 255},${n & 255},${a})`; };

  function draw() {
    const muted = css('--muted'), line = css('--line'), bg = css('--bg');
    ctx.fillStyle = bg; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);
    const s = +el('pc-s').value;
    const path = mode === 'r1' ? rankOnePath(+el('pc-a').value * deg, +el('pc-b').value * deg, s) : rotationPath(+el('pc-th').value * deg);
    const [t0, t1] = mode === 'r1' ? [-1.5, 1.5] : [0, 1];
    const L = 58, R = 18, top = 18, mid = 380, bot = H - 34;
    const X = (t: number) => L + ((t - t0) / (t1 - t0)) * (W - L - R);
    const data = MATERIALS.map((m, i) => (on[i] ? sample(m.W, path, t0, t1, 600) : null));
    // vertical range: shift each curve so its minimum on screen is the reference? keep raw energies, clip at a cap
    let ymax = 0.5;
    data.forEach((d) => { if (d) for (const p of d) if (isFinite(p.w)) ymax = Math.max(ymax, Math.min(p.w, 4)); });
    ymax = Math.min(4, ymax * 1.08);
    const Y = (w: number) => mid - (Math.min(w, ymax) / ymax) * (mid - top);
    ctx.strokeStyle = line; ctx.lineWidth = 1; ctx.font = '13px system-ui, sans-serif'; ctx.fillStyle = muted;
    ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(L, top); ctx.lineTo(L, mid); ctx.lineTo(W - R, mid); ctx.stroke();
    for (const v of [0, ymax / 2, ymax]) ctx.fillText(v.toFixed(1), 8, Y(v) + 4);
    ctx.fillText('W', L + 8, top + 12);
    if (t0 < 0 && t1 > 0) { ctx.setLineDash([3, 4]); ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(X(0), top); ctx.lineTo(X(0), bot); ctx.stroke(); ctx.setLineDash([]); }
    data.forEach((d, i) => {
      if (!d) return;
      ctx.fillStyle = hexA(COLORS[i], 0.16);
      for (let k = 1; k < d.length; k++) {
        const p = d[k], q = d[k - 1];
        if (isFinite(p.w) && isFinite(q.w) && p.k < -1e-3 && q.k < -1e-3) ctx.fillRect(X(q.t), Y(Math.max(p.w, q.w)), X(p.t) - X(q.t) + 0.5, mid - Y(Math.max(p.w, q.w)));
      }
    });
    ctx.save(); ctx.beginPath(); ctx.rect(L, top - 2, W - L - R, mid - top + 2); ctx.clip();
    const Yu = (w: number) => mid - (w / ymax) * (mid - top);
    data.forEach((d, i) => {
      if (!d) return;
      ctx.strokeStyle = COLORS[i]; ctx.lineWidth = 2.5;
      ctx.beginPath(); let pen = false;
      for (const p of d) {
        if (!isFinite(p.w) || p.w > ymax * 4) { pen = false; continue; }
        if (pen) ctx.lineTo(X(p.t), Yu(p.w)); else { ctx.moveTo(X(p.t), Yu(p.w)); pen = true; }
      }
      ctx.stroke();
    });
    ctx.restore();
    const ref = sample(MATERIALS[0].W, path, t0, t1, 600);
    const dtop = mid + 34, dmin = -0.7, dmax = Math.max(2.7, ...ref.map((p) => p.j)) ;
    const D = (j: number) => bot - ((Math.max(dmin, Math.min(dmax, j)) - dmin) / (dmax - dmin)) * (bot - dtop);
    ctx.strokeStyle = line; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(L, dtop); ctx.lineTo(L, bot); ctx.lineTo(W - R, bot); ctx.stroke();
    ctx.setLineDash([3, 4]); ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(L, D(0)); ctx.lineTo(W - R, D(0)); ctx.stroke(); ctx.setLineDash([]);
    ctx.fillStyle = muted; ctx.fillText('det F', 8, dtop + 12); ctx.fillText('0', 40, D(0) + 4);
    ctx.strokeStyle = muted; ctx.lineWidth = 2; ctx.beginPath();
    ref.forEach((p, i) => (i ? ctx.lineTo(X(p.t), D(p.j)) : ctx.moveTo(X(p.t), D(p.j)))); ctx.stroke();
    ctx.fillStyle = muted; ctx.textAlign = 'center';
    for (const t of mode === 'r1' ? [-1.5, -1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5] : [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]) ctx.fillText(String(t), X(t), H - 12);
    ctx.textAlign = 'left'; ctx.fillText('t', W - R - 10, bot - 8);
    readout(data, ref);
  }

  function readout(data: (ReturnType<typeof sample> | null)[], ref: ReturnType<typeof sample>) {
    const s: string[] = [];
    if (mode === 'r1') {
      s.push('Along a rank-one line, det F changes linearly in t.');
      const bad: string[] = [], good: string[] = [];
      data.forEach((d, i) => {
        if (!d) return;
        let m = Infinity; for (const p of d) if (isFinite(p.w) && isFinite(p.k)) m = Math.min(m, p.k);
        (m < -1e-3 ? bad : good).push(`${MATERIALS[i].name} (${m.toFixed(2)})`);
      });
      if (good.length) s.push(`Convex along this line, smallest second derivative in brackets: ${good.join(', ')}.`);
      if (bad.length) s.push(`Not convex along this line: ${bad.join(', ')}.`);
    } else {
      const mid = ref[300];
      s.push(mid.j > 1e-6
        ? `Both ends are rotations, so W = 0 there. At the midpoint det F = ${mid.j.toFixed(2)}, and every energy is higher there than at the ends, so none is convex along this line.`
        : 'Both ends are rotations. The midpoint is a singular matrix (det F = 0), where the energies with a barrier are infinite.');
    }
    (document.getElementById('pc-read') as HTMLElement).textContent = s.join(' ');
  }

  function setMode(m: 'r1' | 'rot') {
    mode = m;
    el('pc-r1').setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(m === 'r1')); el('pc-rot').setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(m === 'rot'));
    (document.getElementById('pc-r1-ctl') as HTMLElement).hidden = m !== 'r1';
    (document.getElementById('pc-rot-ctl') as HTMLElement).hidden = m !== 'rot';
    draw();
  }
  el('pc-r1').addEventListener('click', () => setMode('r1'));
  el('pc-rot').addEventListener('click', () => setMode('rot'));
  for (const [id, out, fmt] of [['pc-a', 'pc-av', (v: string) => v + '°'], ['pc-b', 'pc-bv', (v: string) => v + '°'], ['pc-th', 'pc-thv', (v: string) => v + '°'], ['pc-s', 'pc-sv', (v: string) => (+v).toFixed(2)]] as const) {
    el(id).addEventListener('input', () => { (document.getElementById(out) as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(el(id).value); draw(); });
  }
  matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change', draw);
  draw();
</script>
